Subject: keep long-path Unix sockets at their real path

"docker exec pg psql -U postgres" failed in ThothDock with "connection to
server on socket /var/run/postgresql/.s.PGSQL.5432 failed: No such file or
directory", while TCP worked. A container's rootfs is deep, so the host path
of a socket in it does not fit sun_path (108 bytes). PRoot then bound the
socket at a random short name and remembered the guest path in a binding of
that one PRoot: "docker exec" (another PRoot), a second container sharing
the volume and test -S never found it, and a connect from another PRoot
made up a new random name that did not exist.

Such a socket now lives at its real path, reached through short random
names in PROOT_TMP_DIR (private to the application):

- bind refuses an existing address with EADDRINUSE (like the kernel), binds
  at a random short name prootsock-b-<128 random bits>, and at the
  syscall's exit renames the socket to its real path and leaves the name as
  a symlink to it; if the rename fails, the old per-PRoot binding is kept;
- connect goes through a private random symlink prootsock-c-<128 random
  bits> to the real path, removed at the syscall's exit;
- getsockname, getpeername and accept read the kernel's short name back
  through the symlink, then translate to the guest path;
- symlinks whose socket is gone are removed at the next such bind.

Every name comes from getrandom(2) and is created by bind(2) or symlink(2),
which fail rather than reuse an existing name, so another container cannot
predict, pre-create or collide with one (an earlier draft derived the name
from a hash of the path, which a guest choosing its own path could collide
with). A tracee killed between a connect's entry and exit leaves one
symlink to a live socket behind. The shared-memory listener of patch 0010
and the fallback binding use the same names instead of mktemp.

Only binds and connects of long paths add a ptrace stop (at their exit);
nothing else changes. 32-bit socketcall keeps the old behaviour.

Upstream: termux/proot @ 7266fb3e8516535682f5a9c8f3a7e70f6506eddb (v5.1.107.92), after patches 0001-0013

